---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LpMap from '../../components/LpMap.astro';
import TableHunt from '../../components/TableHunt.astro';
import ScanStats from '../../components/ScanStats.astro';
import RuleReader from '../../components/RuleReader.astro';
import Classifier from '../../components/Classifier.astro';
import Confusion from '../../components/Confusion.astro';
import AugTest from '../../components/AugTest.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
import data from '../../data/lp-cnn.json';
import { PAPER_AUGMENT, allNeighbourhoods } from '../../scripts/lpcnn';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
// The chapter this page is built on lives in the publications collection, so its links stay in one place.
const pubs = (await getCollection('publications')).map((p) => p.data);
const paper = pubs.find((p) => p.id === 'cnn-classification');
const doi = `https://doi.org/${paper?.doi ?? '10.1007/978-3-031-81097-8_3'}`;
const arxiv = paper?.url ?? 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.02740';
const lpPdf = 'https://content.wolfram.com/sites/13/2018/02/04-3-3.pdf';
const pct = (v: number, d = 2) => `${(100 * v).toFixed(d)}%`;
const ring = pct(allNeighbourhoods(64)), line = pct(allNeighbourhoods(64, false));
const scan = data.scan, mine = data.nets;
const n = (v: number) => v.toLocaleString('en');
const conf = mine.class.confusion as number[][], recall = (k: number) => conf[k][k] / conf[k].reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0);
// One tab per step of the argument; the hash picks the tab, so a step can be linked.
const tabs = [ { id: 'question', name: 'The question' }, { id: 'giveaway', name: 'The giveaway' }, { id: 'reading', name: 'Reading the rule' }, { id: 'smaller', name: 'A smaller network' }, { id: 'altered', name: 'Altered pictures' }, { id: 'verdict', name: 'Verdict' },
];
const claim = 'A convolutional network that sorts elementary cellular automata by behaviour can read the rule straight off the pixels, and without precautions it does; a smaller network trained on altered pictures still gets the class right 98 times in 100, but the rule only 61 times: a shift towards the behaviour, not a clean break from the rule.';
---
<Base title="Is the network cheating?" description="What a convolutional network looks at when it sorts cellular automata by behaviour: the rule table hidden in every spacetime diagram, a network that reads it with no training, a smaller one built so that it cannot, and how far altered pictures push it. My chapter in Advances in Cellular Automata, with networks you can run." wide>
  <div class="measure">
    <h1>Is the network cheating?</h1>
    <p>
      An elementary cellular automaton draws a picture. A row of cells, each black or white, is updated over and over by
      one small rule, and each new row goes under the last, so the picture is the automaton's history with time running
      down. Some rules wipe the picture clean, some freeze it, some make it flash, some fill it with noise, and people
      have sorted the 256 rules by these behaviours since the 1980s, by looking. Convolutional neural networks, the
      workhorses of image recognition, have been trained to do the same from the pictures and score 98 to 100 percent.
      My question was what such a network is actually looking at, because a spacetime diagram carries more than its
      behaviour: it also carries the rule that drew it, written out in its pixels.
    </p>
    <p>
      The answer is in a <a href={doi}>book chapter</a> with Aisling Daly and Jan Baetens, in <em>Advances in Cellular
      Automata</em>, volume 2 (Springer, 2025). In one sentence:
    </p>
    <p class="claim">{claim}</p>
    <p>
      The tabs follow the chapter: the classes and the question, how a diagram gives its rule away, a network that reads
      the rule without any training, a smaller network built so that it cannot, the altered pictures that push it
      further, and how far all that gets.
    </p>
    <p>
      The chapter's code is not public yet; it will be linked here. Until then, the networks you can run on this page are
      a retraining of the chapter's final network, done for this page with the chapter's data recipe, layers, training
      settings and alterations, keeping the best of three runs as the chapter kept the best of a few. Where its
      accuracies differ from the chapter's, both are given. The chapter's own numbers are quoted as published, the scan
      statistics come from the same retraining script, and the figures compute everything else in your browser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabbed">
    <nav class="tabs" aria-label="Steps">
      <ul role="tablist" aria-orientation="vertical">
        {tabs.map((t, i) => <li><a role="tab" id={`tab-${t.id}`} href={`#${t.id}`} aria-controls={t.id} aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1}>{t.name}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="panels">

      <section role="tabpanel" id="question" aria-labelledby="tab-question" tabindex="0">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>The question</h2>
        <p>
          Elementary is the smallest interesting case: two states, and each cell updated from itself and its two
          neighbours. A rule is then a table of eight entries, one per neighbourhood from 000 to 111, so there are <Tex tex="2^8 = 256" /> rules, each numbered by reading its table as a binary number. Wolfram's four classes are
          the best-known way of sorting them, but they are judgement calls: the same rule can land in different classes
          depending on who looks, and at which start. The chapter uses the five classes of Li and Packard (1990) instead,
          which give every rule exactly one class. A picture's class is then fixed by its rule, which is what a training
          set of labelled pictures needs.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><em>Null</em>, 24 rules: everything turns one colour.</li>
          <li><em>Fixed point</em>, 97 rules: the picture settles into a pattern that stays put, or shifts sideways.</li>
          <li><em>Periodic</em>, 89 rules: it repeats every two or three steps.</li>
          <li><em>Locally chaotic</em>, 10 rules: chaos, fenced in by walls that do not move.</li>
          <li><em>Chaotic</em>, 36 rules: noise everywhere, or differences that spread.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Two symmetries never change the class: reading the rule in a mirror, and swapping black and white. Rule 110
          seen in a mirror is rule 124, with its colours swapped it is rule 137, with both it is rule 193, and all four
          are chaotic. Li and Packard count them as chaotic, though 110 is also famously complex. The two symmetries fold
          the 256 rules into 88 classes of equivalent rules. Click around the map; the rule you pick is ringed, its
          equivalents get a dashed ring.
        </p>
      </div>
      <LpMap />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          You can name most of these at a glance, and so, it seemed, could a convolutional network. Silverman (2019)
          trained one on Wolfram's classes and scored 100 percent on his test set; Comelli and colleagues (2021) trained
          one for eleven different classifications and reached 97.58 percent on Li and Packard's. The trouble is that a
          classification is easy if you know the rule, and the picture can tell you the rule. So the chapter makes a
          distinction. A feature of a diagram is <em>microscopic</em> if it fits inside one neighbourhood together with
          its next state, a window of 3 by 2 cells, and <em>mesoscopic</em> if it spans many cells and many steps:
          stripes, gliders, walls between regions that behave differently. A person classifies by the mesoscopic
          features. Which ones does the network use, and can it be pushed from the first kind to the second?
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="giveaway" aria-labelledby="tab-giveaway" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>The giveaway</h2>
        <p>
          Take three neighbouring cells in a row and the cell below the middle one. The shape is a T-tetromino, and it
          is one line of the rule table, read straight off the picture: this neighbourhood, this next state. Slide the T
          along the first row and every neighbourhood it has not met yet fills in another line. Press play, or step
          through the scan one cell at a time:
        </p>
      </div>
      <TableHunt />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Every rule has one class, so the scan classifies. How often does it fall short? A row of 64 random cells,
          wrapped round as the automaton's row is, contains all eight neighbourhoods with probability {ring}. That number
          is exact: it counts the rows that leave out a given set of neighbourhoods as walks on the graph whose four
          nodes are pairs of cells, and adds and subtracts those counts over all 256 sets. The chapter says "over 99
          percent"; without the wrap it would be {line}, just under. The 63 rows that follow only add chances, unless the
          rule wipes them out. On a fresh draw of the chapter's training set, 1024 diagrams of 64 by 64 for every rule:
        </p>
      </div>
      <ScanStats />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The rules that hide something are the ones that destroy information: null rules erase the start after a
          step or two, fixed-point rules freeze it, while chaotic rules keep producing every neighbourhood. When an
          entry is missing it is nearly always 000 or 111, three equal cells in a row, which some rules simply never make
          after the first row. The chapter shows two such diagrams: one of rule 59, periodic, without a single run of three
          white cells, and one of rule 18, chaotic, without three black ones. Such diagrams are rare, but trying
          start after start turns one of each up: <a href="?hunt.rule=59&hunt.seed=5410#giveaway">rule 59</a> and <a href="?hunt.rule=18&hunt.seed=451#giveaway">rule 18</a>. One
          missing entry leaves two candidate rules, and they nearly always share a class. The chapter found a single
          diagram among its 262,144 where they did not: rule 59 without its 000 could pass for rule 58, which is fixed
          point.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          A note on the counting, added for this page. The chapter counts 370 incomplete diagrams in its training set and
          33 in its test set. Its scan padded each row with a copy of the row's own first and last cell instead of
          wrapping it round, which adds two windows per row. With that padding the fresh draw above has {n(scan.trainval_edge.incomplete)} incomplete diagrams, about the chapter's count; wrapped round, as the
          automaton is, it has {n(scan.trainval_wrap.incomplete)}, about one diagram in {Math.round(scan.trainval_wrap.diagrams / scan.trainval_wrap.incomplete)}, and in {scan.trainval_wrap.ambiguous} of those the missing entry leaves the class undecided, against the chapter's one. Either way the scan
          reads the rule from all but a few hundred of a quarter of a million diagrams.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="reading" aria-labelledby="tab-reading" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Reading the rule</h2>
        <p>
          The chapter first rebuilt the network of the earlier work: two convolutional layers of 16 channels each, with
          filters 3 cells wide and 2 high, then a global max pool that keeps only each channel's largest value, a dense
          layer of 256 nodes and five outputs, 7,301 parameters in all. Trained on 196,608 diagrams, it got the class
          wrong in 6 of the 65,536 it had not seen, 99.99 percent right, and all six were borderline cases between null,
          fixed point and periodic. Then it was asked for the rule instead of the class, and it named the exact rule in
          99.85 percent of the unseen diagrams. A near-perfect class score is therefore no evidence of looking at
          behaviour. The network can know the rule.
        </p>
        <p>
          How it can know is easy to write down, because a 3 × 2 filter fits exactly one T-tetromino. Put +1 on each of
          the three upper cells where the neighbourhood has a 1, −1 where it has a 0, +1 on the cell below, and set the
          bias to minus the number of 1s in the neighbourhood. For the neighbourhood 110 followed by a 1 that is the
          filter
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \quad \text{bias } -2," />
        <p>
          and its sum is 1 exactly where that T-tetromino sits, and 0 or less anywhere else. After a ReLU the channel is
          a map of the places where 110 was followed by a 1. Eight such detectors, the global maximum of each, and the
          eight maxima are the rule table: a 1 for every neighbourhood that is followed by a 1 somewhere in the picture.
          A last layer of 256 nodes turns them into a rule number with fixed weights. Node <Tex tex="i" /> has weights
          and bias
        </p>
        <Tex block tex="w_{i,j} = 2\,\mathrm{bin}(i)_j - 1, \qquad b_i = 1 - \sum_{j=0}^{7} \mathrm{bin}(i)_j," />
        <p>
          with <Tex tex="\mathrm{bin}(i)_j" /> the <Tex tex="j" />th binary digit of <Tex tex="i" />, so it scores 1 minus
          the number of entries in which rule <Tex tex="i" /> differs from what the detectors saw, and the top node is
          the rule that was read. Nothing is learned; the network is 2,360 numbers, all written down. On the chapter's
          262,144 diagrams it named the rule in 99.87 percent of them. Its 331 errors are diagrams with a missing entry:
          a neighbourhood that never occurs leaves its channel empty, which reads as a 0, and that is wrong whenever the
          true entry is a 1. On the fresh draw of the previous tab, wrapped round, the reader also gets exactly {n(scan.trainval_wrap.handsetWrong)} wrong.
        </p>
      </div>
      <RuleReader />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Things to try. Rule 120 is the chapter's Fig. 8.8: four channels light up, and 01111000 is 120 in binary.
          Invert the colours of any diagram and the reader reads the complement; mirror it and it reads the mirror image.
          Either way the class it then names is still right, since equivalent rules share their class, so the trick
          survives two of the alterations of a later tab untouched. Coarse-graining and noise are another matter. The
          detectors then fire at odd heights, or where the rule never put a T-tetromino, and what comes out is usually
          another rule altogether.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="smaller" aria-labelledby="tab-smaller" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>A smaller network</h2>
        <p>
          If a network can read the rule, training will find out, since it is the shortest way to a perfect score. So
          the chapter takes the possibility away, by three principles. The first filters are too small to hold a
          T-tetromino, 2 × 2 instead of 3 × 2, and there are four channels, fewer than the eight neighbourhoods. The
          network must see far enough to take in a stripe or a glider: a max pool halfway widens what one unit of the
          last layer sees to 8 by 8 cells, against 5 by 3 for the default network. And after the global pool only four
          numbers remain, feeding a dense layer of 16 nodes that has little room to store anything about 256 rules.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">layer</th><th scope="col">output</th><th scope="col">parameters</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">the diagram</th><td>64 × 64</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">2 × 2 convolution, 4 channels, ReLU</th><td>4 × 63 × 63</td><td>20</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">2 × 2 convolution, 4 channels, ReLU</th><td>4 × 62 × 62</td><td>68</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">2 × 2 max pool</th><td>4 × 31 × 31</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">2 × 2 convolution, 4 channels, ReLU</th><td>4 × 30 × 30</td><td>68</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">2 × 2 convolution, 4 channels, ReLU</th><td>4 × 29 × 29</td><td>68</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">global max pool</th><td>4</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">dense, 16 nodes, no activation</th><td>16</td><td>80</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">dense, softmax</th><td>5 classes</td><td>85</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">total</th><td></td><td>389</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          To name the rule instead, the two dense layers become a single softmax layer of 256 nodes (1,504 parameters),
          or of 88 nodes, one per class of equivalent rules (664 parameters). Untouched by any alteration, this network
          gets the class of 99.10 percent of the validation diagrams right. Asked for the rule, it gets 71.19 percent:
          far below the default network's 99.85, and far above the 0.4 percent of a guess. Small filters slow the
          reading down without preventing it, because two 2 × 2 filters in a row see 3 × 3 cells, enough for a
          T-tetromino.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below are the final networks, trained with the alterations of the next tab. Everything they say comes from
          the networks running in your browser. The boxes on the diagram are the 8 × 8 windows that win the global pool,
          one per channel of the last layer. They are the only parts of the picture that reach the output.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Classifier />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Things to try, on the first start of each rule. Rule 54, the default, is complex in Wolfram's sense and chaotic
          in Li and Packard's. The class network is sure of it whatever you do to the picture, coarse-graining included;
          the rule network names its class of equivalent rules on the plain picture and loses it once the picture is
          inverted or mirrored, although neither changes the class it was trained to name; and the reader reads the
          complement, 147, on the inverted picture and rule 1 on the coarse-grained one. Rule 110 goes the other way
          round: the rule network misses it on the plain picture and finds it on all three altered ones. The locally
          chaotic rules 73 and 26 are where the class network is least sure of itself; try a few starts and alterations
          and watch it waver.
        </p>
        <p class="meta status-line">
          This retraining: {pct(mine.class.test)} of {n(32768)} fresh test diagrams in the right class, against the
          chapter's 98.17%, and {pct(mine.indep.test)} in the right class of equivalent rules, against 61.25%; trained
          with batches of 64, Adam at a learning rate of 0.001 and early stopping on {n(65536)} validation diagrams, by
          scripts/train-lp-cnn.py in this site's repository.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="altered" aria-labelledby="tab-altered" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Altered pictures</h2>
        <p>
          The other lever is the data. Alter each picture in a way that leaves its behaviour as a person would describe
          it but changes the pixels, keep the label, and a network that leans on the pixels is punished. The usual reason
          to alter training data is to have more of it; here there is no shortage, since every random start gives a new
          diagram, so the purpose is to make the rule less useful than the behaviour. The chapter tries four alterations:
          swapping black and white, mirroring left and right, coarse-graining by replacing each 2 × 2 block with its
          average grey, and salt-and-pepper noise, a share of cells set to black or white at random. During training,
          each diagram of a batch gets one of the <Tex tex="n" /> chosen alterations with probability <Tex tex="n/(n+1)" /> and stays as it is otherwise. All four are in the menus of the figures on the previous
          tabs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inverting and mirroring turn a rule into an equivalent one with the same class. The class label stays true,
          while the rule label no longer matches the picture shown. Mirroring often changes nothing at all, since 64
          rules are their own mirror image; only 16 are their own complement, so inversion costs the rule more.
          Coarse-graining breaks every T-tetromino, and noise writes contradictions into the table. The highest
          validation accuracies the chapter reached, each trained separately (Tab. 8.3):
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="aug">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">alteration</th><th scope="col">rule, of 256</th><th scope="col">up to symmetry, of 88</th><th scope="col">class, of 5</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {PAPER_AUGMENT.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{r.name}</th>
                  {[r.rule, r.indep, r.cls].map((v, k) => <td><span class="bar" style={`--w:${v}%`} data-k={k}></span>{v.toFixed(2)}%</td>)}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Every alteration costs some accuracy, the class least. Coarse-graining opens the widest gap: the rule falls to
          fewer than one diagram in six while the class stays near 98 percent. Noise lowered everything without
          favouring the class, so it was dropped; all four together made training harder than any one alone. Inversion
          and mirroring leave the 88-way task almost untouched, or even help it, which is what you would expect of
          alterations that move a rule within its own class of equivalents.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same comparison can be run on this page's networks, next to the hand-set reader of the "Reading the rule"
          tab, on fresh diagrams in your browser:
        </p>
      </div>
      <AugTest />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          On untouched, inverted or mirrored pictures the reader beats both networks at both tasks: it gets the class
          every time and the rule up to symmetry nearly every time, where the rule network manages about 60 percent.
          Inversion and mirroring hide nothing from a reader built for them. Coarse-graining does: the reader falls to
          about one class in five and almost no rules, while the class network still gets close to nine classes in ten.
          Noise, which neither network saw in training, hurts everything, the reader most. The trained networks are worse
          than the reader at what the reader does, and their advantage shows only where the rule table has been
          destroyed. Whether what they use instead is the behaviour a person sees is exactly the question accuracy alone
          cannot answer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two common alterations were deliberately left out: shifting left or right, which a stack of convolutions
          followed by a global maximum ignores anyway, and rotation, which swaps space and time and makes a picture that
          is no automaton's diagram. The chapter says plainly that it did not check either expectation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The final network was trained on all 262,144 diagrams with inversion, mirroring and coarse-graining. It puts
          98.17 percent of the test diagrams in the right class. The same architecture, trained the same way with 88
          outputs, names the rule up to symmetry for 61.25 percent of them. From the default network to this one, rule
          recovery fell from over 99 percent to 61 while class accuracy lost less than two points.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

      <section role="tabpanel" id="verdict" aria-labelledby="tab-verdict" tabindex="0" hidden>
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>What it adds up to</h2>
        <p>
          Where the final network goes wrong (Fig. 8.11), next to the retraining that runs on this page:
        </p>
      </div>
      <Confusion />
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Read along the rows, four classes are recognised between 97.9 and 99.0 percent of the time, and the locally
          chaotic one 92.8 percent: it is the smallest class, under 4 percent of the diagrams. The most common mistake is
          a periodic diagram taken for a fixed point, 219 times: a flash with a period of two looks like stillness to a
          network that pools over the whole picture and barely registers time. Read down the columns, as the chapter's
          figure does, and 5 percent of the diagrams called locally chaotic were in fact chaotic, though only 1.4 percent
          of the chaotic ones were called locally chaotic. The retraining errs in much the same way: a periodic diagram
          taken for a fixed point is again its most common mistake ({conf[2][1]} diagrams), and the locally chaotic class
          is again the hardest, recognised {pct(recall(3), 1)} of the time and taken for periodic more often than by the
          chapter's network.
        </p>
        <p>
          The chapter's answer comes in two halves. Without precautions a network relies on the rule, and earlier work
          overlooked this: the high accuracies in the literature are not, on their own, evidence of pattern recognition.
          Can the network be pushed towards the behaviour? A qualified yes. The chapter's main conclusion:
        </p>
        <p class="claim">{claim}</p>
        <p>
          The chapter also names its own limits. No rule was held out: every rule was in the training set and the test
          set differed only in its starting rows, so the 98 percent measures how well the network handles new pictures
          of familiar rules, not new rules, which is what the motivation asks for. Holding out whole classes of equivalent
          rules is the obvious next experiment. The class and rule accuracies come from separately trained networks, one
          with five outputs and one with 88; relative to chance, 61 percent out of 88 options is the stronger result.
          And the small architecture was chosen by judgement, as the best of a few runs, with no estimate of the spread.
          The lesson about method stands regardless: a classifier of automaton behaviour should be judged on how well it
          recovers the rule as well as the class, with the rule-table scan as the baseline.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond elementary rules, the classes still make sense for any black-and-white diagram of a row of cells, such
          as those of non-uniform automata, where each cell has its own rule; the network that simulates those is on <a href="/demos/nuca-cnn/">a page of its own</a>. Discovering classes that the elementary rules never show is a
          harder problem, because a network trained to separate five known classes has no reason to keep room for a sixth.
          The chapter is also Chapter 8 of <a href="/demos/thesis/#cheating">my thesis</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Read more</h2>
        <ul class="pubs">
          <li>
            <a href={doi}>Convolutional neural networks for automated cellular automaton classification</a>
            <div class="meta"><strong>M. Rollier</strong>, A. J. Daly, J. M. Baetens</div>
            <div class="meta">Advances in Cellular Automata, vol. 2 (Springer), pp. 49–70, 2025 · <a href={arxiv}>preprint</a></div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={lpPdf}>The structure of the elementary cellular automata rule space</a>
            <div class="meta">W. Li and N. Packard, Complex Systems 4, 281–297, 1990. The five classes; Table 2 gives the class of every rule.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00175">Convolutional neural networks for cellular automata classification</a>
            <div class="meta">E. Silverman, Artificial Life Conference Proceedings, 280–282, 2019. The network the chapter starts from.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.5220/0010160004670474">Comparing elementary cellular automata classifications with a convolutional neural network</a>
            <div class="meta">T. Comelli, F. Pinel and P. Bouvry, ICAART 2021, 467–474. Eleven classifications, one network.</div>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="meta">
          Keys once a figure has focus: the arrows step through the rules, n draws a new start, f goes fullscreen. The
          address bar follows the settings, so a view can be linked.
        </p>
      </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

